  "typed": "If the brain can’t feel pain, why do headaches hurt?",
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 1.68,
      "text": "Your brain helps you feel pain."
    },
    {
      "start": 1.645,
      "end": 4.78,
      "text": "But brain tissue itself has no pain sensors."
    },
    {
      "start": 4.745,
      "end": 6.83,
      "text": "The lining and nerves around it are different."
    },
    {
      "start": 7.225,
      "end": 8.4,
      "text": "Look closer at the lining."
    },
    {
      "start": 8.365,
      "end": 10.64,
      "text": "It has pain-sensitive nerve endings."
    },
    {
      "start": 10.605,
      "end": 11.84,
      "text": "When these nerves are activated,"
    },
    {
      "start": 11.805,
      "end": 13.71,
      "text": "they send a message toward the brain."
    },
    {
      "start": 14.165,
      "end": 15.58,
      "text": "The message reaches your brain,"
    },
    {
      "start": 15.545,
      "end": 17.66,
      "text": "which processes it as pain."
    },
    {
      "start": 17.625,
      "end": 20.18,
      "text": "That’s why a headache can feel deep inside your head,"
    },
    {
      "start": 20.145,
      "end": 22.86,
      "text": "even when the signal started in nearby tissue."
    },
    {
      "start": 22.825,
      "end": 24.11,
      "text": "Different headaches have different causes."
    }
  ],
